'''''[[Strictly Come Dancing]]'''''  returned for its '''eighteenth series''' with a launch show on 17 October 2020 on BBC One, and the live shows beginning on 24 October.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.radiotimes.com/news/tv/2020-10-01/strictly-come-dancing-2020-launch-sat-17th-october/|title=Strictly Come Dancing to launch on Saturday 17th October|work=[[Radio Times]]|first=Patrick|last=McLean|date=1 October 2020|access-date=1 October 2020|language=en-GB}}</ref> Due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], the series launched a month later than usual and ran for only nine weeks instead of the usual thirteen.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-53168872|title=Strictly Come Dancing 2020 to be shorter series|work=BBC News|date=24 June 2020|access-date=24 June 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.radiotimes.com/news/tv/2020-07-01/strictly-come-dancing-2020-start-date-blackpool/|title=Strictly start date revealed as Blackpool set to go ahead during shorter series|first=David|last=Craig|work=[[Radio Times]]|date=1 July 2020|access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://tellymix.co.uk/reality-tv/strictly-come-dancing/446035-strictly-come-dancing-2020-start-date-revealed-with-a-shorter-series.html|title=Strictly Come Dancing 2020 start date 'revealed' with a shorter series|first=Josh|last=Darvill|work=TellyMix|date=1 July 2020|access-date=24 August 2020}}</ref> [[Tess Daly]] and [[Claudia Winkleman]] returned as hosts, while [[Zoe Ball]] and [[Rylan Clark-Neal]] returned to host ''[[Strictly Come Dancing: It Takes Two]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://metro.co.uk/2020/09/02/how-strictly-come-dancing-2020-going-to-work-13211904/|title=How is Strictly Come Dancing 2020 going to work?|work=[[Metro (British newspaper)|Metro]]|first=Aidan|last=Milan|date=2 September 2020|access-date=18 October 2020|language=en-GB}}</ref> Clark-Neal was replaced by [[Gethin Jones]] for four shows after coming into contact with someone who had tested positive for COVID-19.

On 12 September, it was announced that for the first time since the [[Strictly Come Dancing (series 10)|tenth series]], there would be no live show broadcast from the [[Blackpool Tower Ballroom]] due to the pandemic.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-54134368|title=Strictly's Blackpool special cancelled for 2020|date=12 September 2020|access-date=12 September 2020|publisher=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> However, it was announced shortly afterwards that there would be four episodes highlighting the best moments from previous series.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/newsround/54092487|title=Strictly Come Dancing: The Best Of series starts in September – CBBC Newsround|publisher=[[BBC]]|date=19 September 2020|access-date=19 September 2020}}</ref> The final episode featured a tribute to 2014 winner [[Caroline Flack]], who had died on 15 February that year.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.digitalspy.com/tv/reality-tv/a34336189/strictly-come-dancing-caroline-flack-tribute/|title=Strictly Come Dancing pays an emotional tribute to Caroline Flack|first=Tasha|last=Hegarty|date=10 October 2020|access-date=10 October 2020|website=[[Digital Spy]]}}</ref>

[[Shirley Ballas]], [[Craig Revel Horwood]], and [[Motsi Mabuse]] returned to the judging panel.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/entertainment/film-tv/news/in-pictures-a-first-look-at-the-strictly-come-dancing-launch-show-39634651.html|title=In Pictures: A first look at the Strictly Come Dancing launch show|work=[[Belfast Telegraph]]|first=Kerri-Ann|last=Ropper|date=16 October 2020|access-date=18 October 2020|language=en-GB}}</ref> On 21 August 2020, it was announced that [[Bruno Tonioli]] could not be part of the judging panel for this series due to travel restrictions imposed by the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] and his role on ''[[Dancing with the Stars (American TV series)|Dancing with the Stars]]''. Tonioli instead appeared virtually to give his critiques on the performances during the Sunday night results show. Motsi Mabuse had to miss weeks 4 and 5 in accordance with British government guidelines after travelling to Germany due to a break-in at her dance academy;<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/MOTSI_MABUSE/status/1326992505037053953|title=Motsi Mabuse on Twitter: "#Strictly"|date=12 November 2020|access-date=13 November 2020}}</ref><ref name="hp">{{cite web|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/motsi-mabuse-strictly-come-dancing-dance-school-break-in-germany_uk_5fafa457c5b6c5f3d2f7a6bc|title=Strictly Come Dancing's Motsi Mabuse Reveals Break-In At Her Dance School, Amid Her Return To Germany|access-date=28 November 2020|date=14 November 2020|work=[[Huffington Post]]}}</ref> she was replaced by professional dancer [[Anton Du Beke]], who had previously been eliminated from the competition alongside [[Jacqui Smith]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/bbcstrictly/status/1327221330039754753|title=BBC Strictly on Twitter: "News: Anton has a new role on #Strictly this weekend!"|date=13 November 2020|access-date=13 November 2020}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-54930079|title=Strictly Come Dancing: Anton Du Beke to replace Motsi Mabuse this weekend|publisher=[[BBC News]]|date=13 November 2020|access-date=14 November 2020|language=en-GB}}</ref> Du Beke became a permanent judge from [[Strictly Come Dancing series 19|the nineteenth series]].

[[Bill Bailey]] and [[Oti Mabuse]] were announced as the winners on 19 December,<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 December 2020 |title=Strictly Come Dancing: Bill Bailey crowned 2020 winner |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-55345136 |access-date=19 December 2020 |publisher=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> while [[Hrvy|HRVY]] and [[Janette Manrara]], [[Jamie Laing]] and [[Karen Hauer]], and [[Maisie Smith]] and [[Gorka Márquez]] were the runners-up.

